0) Project Planning and Preparation
Successful programmers spend time planning before they begin coding. After reviewing
the project requirements, answer the following questions to help guide your design and
development process.
A. What concepts from class or previous assignments might help you complete this
project? Which programming concepts (loops, conditionals, lists, classes, etc.) do
you expect to use? What programming skills do you want to improve while
completing this project?
Drafter intro, loops, conditionals, dataclasses.
B. Which parts of the project do you expect to be the easiest for you? The hardest?
Why?
The dataclasses, the hardest part i think will be the drafter implementations
C. Even though you have not yet designed the final project, estimate which tasks
will likely require the most time and effort? Which features or components should
be implemented first?
Defining the functions and creating a working drafter site
D. What distractions might interfere with your work and how will you minimize them?
What resources will you need available while working? What questions, if any, do
you still have about the assignment requirements?
Distractions may be struggling with the assignment and trying to put it off til later
1) Site Diagram
Create a mock-up of your planned website. We suggest you make the mockup on
paper, although you can also use software like PowerPoint or Slides to make a mockup.
There is other design software that could also be used, but we strongly encourage you
to keep it simple. Do not spend more than 15-30 minutes detailing the mock-up. Your
goal should be to make a very quick and dirty diagram, to show that you have some
kind of idea.
We recommend drawing each page as a rectangle, and laying out the text, buttons, and
other components inside. Then, use arrows to indicate links between pages. Label each
page the way you will name each function, and then put labels on the arrows to indicate
what the button links. Also write down any parameters that should come into the pages.
In addition to the pages and routes, write down what fields your `State` will have, both
their names and types. Think clearly about what data will need to be persistent across
the entire website.
Add photo(s) or screenshot(s) of your website mock-up and labeled diagram on the next
page.
